#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# Lists and data

#% text
# Lists, `table`, `map`, `select`, associations and `group_by` cover most data handling; `import_data` and `export_data` read and write files.

#% section
# Building and transforming lists

#% cell
table(k^2, k, 8)
#% output
#= [1, 4, 9, 16, 25, 36, 49, 64]

#% cell
map(x => x^2 + 1, [1, 2, 3])
#% output
#= [2, 5, 10]

#% cell
select([1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6], x => x > 3)
#% output
#= [4, 5, 6]

#% cell
sort_by(["pear", "fig", "banana"], s => string_length(s))
#% output
#= ["fig", "pear", "banana"]

#% cell
total([1, 2, 3, 4])
#% output
#= 10

#% text
# Elements are picked with `[k]` (counting from 1); `length`, `sort`, `reverse` and `take` do what they say.

#% cell
[10, 20, 30][2]
#% output
#= 20

#% cell
take(sort([3, 1, 2, 5, 4]), 3)
#% output
#= [1, 2, 3]

#% section
# Iterator lists in `table`

#% text
# The range of a `table` can be a count, a list of values (`range(a, b)` makes one), or a bracketed `[k, a, b]` with an optional step; the bounds may be any numbers. Several variables nest, a later range may use an earlier variable.

#% cell
table(k^2, k, range(1, 8))
#% output
#= [1, 4, 9, 16, 25, 36, 49, 64]

#% cell
table(x^i, i, [1, 3, 5])
#% output
#= [x, x^3, x^5]

#% cell
table(k, [k, 1.5, 4])
#% output
#= [1.5, 2.5, 3.5]

#% cell
table(k, [k, 10, 1, -3])
#% output
#= [10, 7, 4, 1]

#% cell
table(i + j, i, 3, j, i)
#% output
#= [[2], [3, 4], [4, 5, 6]]

#% cell
table(i*j, i, 3, j, 3)
#% output
#= [[1, 2, 3], [2, 4, 6], [3, 6, 9]]

#% section
# Arithmetic on every element: `elementwise` and `threaded`

#% text
# `elementwise(f)(a, b, ...)` applies `f` to the corresponding elements of lists at every level; an argument that is not a list is used with every element. `threaded(v)` places `v` along the last levels of the other arguments instead, so a row is combined with every row of a matrix.

#% cell
elementwise(max)([1, -1, 3, -4, 5], 0)
#% output
#= [1, 0, 3, 0, 5]

#% cell
elementwise(f)([a, b], [1, 2])
#% output
#= [f(a, 1), f(b, 2)]

#% cell
elementwise(f)([[1, 2, 3], [4, 5, 6]], [a, b])
#% output
#= [[f(1, a), f(2, a), f(3, a)], [f(4, b), f(5, b), f(6, b)]]

#% cell
elementwise(f)([[1, 2, 3], [4, 5, 6]], threaded([a, b, c]))
#% output
#= [[f(1, a), f(2, b), f(3, c)], [f(4, a), f(5, b), f(6, c)]]

#% text
# Arithmetic spreads a threaded operand the same way, so `+` and `*` need no `elementwise`. A second argument to `threaded` chooses the level where `v` starts, and the values of associations are paired too.

#% cell
[[1, 2], [3, 4]] + threaded([x, y])
#% output
#= [[1 + x, 2 + y], [3 + x, 4 + y]]

#% cell
elementwise(add)([[1, 2], [3, 4]], threaded([x, y], 1))
#% output
#= [[1 + x, 2 + x], [3 + y, 4 + y]]

#% cell
elementwise(f)(assoc(a -> 1, b -> 2), 3)
#% output
#= assoc(a -> f(1, 3), b -> f(2, 3))

#% section
# Associations and grouping

#% text
# An association maps keys to values: `assoc(key -> value, ...)`, read with `h[key]`; `keys` and `values` list the two sides. `group_by` builds one from a list; `merge` combines several, a later value replacing an earlier one.

#% cell
h = assoc("a" -> 1, "b" -> 2); [h["a"], keys(h), values(h)]
#% output
#= [1, ["a", "b"], [1, 2]]

#% cell
group_by(range(1, 10), x => mod(x, 3))
#% output
#= assoc(1 -> [1, 4, 7, 10], 2 -> [2, 5, 8], 0 -> [3, 6, 9])

#% cell
merge([assoc("a" -> 1, "b" -> 2), assoc("b" -> 3)])
#% output
#= assoc("a" -> 1, "b" -> 3)

#% section
# Files

#% cell
export_data("points.csv", [[1, 2], [3, 4]])
#% output
#= "points.csv"

#% cell
import_data("points.csv")
#% output
#= [[1, 2], [3, 4]]

#% section
# Common mistakes

#% item
# Arithmetic is element by element on lists of equal length; mixed lengths are an error.

#% cell
[1, 2, 3] + [1, 2]
#% output
#! failure(shape_mismatch)

#% item
# `table(e, k, n)` runs `k` from 1 to `n`. A fourth argument starts a second, nested loop, so `table(k^2, k, 3, 8)` is not the squares of 3 to 8: the range of `k` is 3, and the engine warns. For a range that does not start at 1 write `table(k^2, [k, 3, 8])` or `table(k^2, k, range(3, 8))`.

#% cell
table(k^2, [k, 3, 8])
#% output
#= [9, 16, 25, 36, 49, 64]

#% item
# A matrix plus a vector pairs the vector with the rows, one element for each row. To add the vector to every row, mark it `threaded`.

#% cell
[[1, 2], [3, 4]] + [10, 20]
#% output
#= [[11, 12], [23, 24]]

#% cell
[[1, 2], [3, 4]] + threaded([10, 20])
#% output
#= [[11, 22], [13, 24]]

#% section
# Related functions

#% text
# `table`, `map`, `select`, `sort_by`, `group_by`, `merge`, `elementwise`, `threaded`, `import_data`, `export_data`
